TI  - SYSTEMATIC ANALYSIS OF 95 RECIPROCAL TRANSLOCATIONS OF AUTOSOMES
AB  - A statistical analysis of 95 cases of reciprocal translocations involving autosomes detected among about 10,000 patients was studied with the R[reverse]-banding technique. An excess of break points existed for chromosome arms 4p, 9p, 10q, 21q and 22q and a deficiency existed for 1p, 2p and 6q. There were relatively more break points in the small arms than in the large arms, when the translocation was ascertained through an unbalanced translocation carrier. Except for chromosome 22, an ascertainment bias explained this non-random distribution. An excess of telomeric break points existed in all cases of translocations ascertained through unbalanced carriers, and an excess of centromeric break point existed in the case of 3:1 and 1:3 segregations only. These excesses were explained by an ascertainment bias. The break points were located usually at the junction of the bands (interfaces). The size of the chromosomal imbalance varied in the ascertainment classes. It was very large in cases ascertained through balanced carriers (at least one break point was far from the telomere), large in cases ascertained through abortion and relatively moderate in cases ascertained through unbalanced translocation carriers (at least 1 break point was juxta telomeric). An excess of balanced reciprocal translocations existed in the sample of mentally retarded and malformed children existed. This may be due to a position effect. An excess of balanced reciprocal translocations (not involving chromosome 21) existed among the trisomics 21 and their parents. An interchromosomal effect is suggested. A large excess of maternal transmission exists in cases of 3:1 segregation of reciprocal translocation.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
AU  - LINTS, FA
AU  - HOSTE, C
TI  - LANSING EFFECT REVISITED .2. CUMULATIVE AND SPONTANEOUSLY REVERSIBLE PARENTAL AGE EFFECTS ON FECUNDITY IN DROSOPHILA-MELANOGASTER
AB  - In D. melanogaster the wild strain Cugo was reproduced for 10 generations both at a young and at an old age. There were 3 replicate stocks, subjected respectively to high, low or no selection for abdominal bristle number. The mean total fecundity of all lines dropped regularly, although the rate of decrease depended on the parental age at reproduction, to around 20% the original wild strain fecundity and was restored afterwards at values approaching the original, and then probably remained stabilized. Modifications of the mean daily egg production, in the maximal daily egg production and of the day where the maximal egg production occurred were observed. The results parallel those of Lansing in that the continuous reproduction from both young and old parents alters to a considerable extent various parameters related to fecundity, and that the effects of such systems of reproduction are cumulative, reversible, and due neither to classical genic nor to environmental factors. Those results differ from Lansing''s data on 3 main points: decrease in total fecundity is progressive; accumulation of parental age effects does not culminate in the extinction of the lines; and the reversibility of the parental age effects is not induced by a change in the age at which reproduction occurs but by a re-equilibration mechanism present in the strain itself. The possible mechanisms of the parental age and Lansing effects are analyzed and the bearings of the present results on the evolutionary theory of senescence are discussed.
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
AU  - WATTIAUX, JM
TI  - CUMULATIVE PARENTAL AGE EFFECTS IN DROSOPHILA SUBOBSCURA
AB  - From a sample of D. subobscura collected in a natural biotope, 2 lines were derived. The first, called T line, was reproduced only through young individuals (3 to 10 days old). The second, B line, used only parents from 6 to 8 weeks old. Sexual behavior was observed by recording individual copulations during 4 or 6 hr. T and B lines were mated at 3 different periods of the selection experiment. Male sexual activity increases considerably in the T line but shows an opposite tendency in the B line. Female sexual activity varies only slightly in the 2 lines. A decrease of longevity and fecundity is observed in T flies. Greater fecundity at more advanced ages is exhibited by B flies, with respect to the original samples. The 2 lines show a considerable divergence in the frequency of gene arrangements. The A1 and O3+4+8 inversions are particularly concerned. The index of heterozygosity and the recombination index also differ significantly. The role of natural selection and a specific, cumulative, Lansing effect independent from natural selection is discussed; however, no definite evidence is available in favor of either hypothesis.
ER  -

